<i data-commentator="Be'er HaGolah" data-label="י" data-order="10"></i> Some authorities hold that &lt;i&gt;Parashas <i data-commentator="Mishnah Berurah" data-label="יד"></i> Zachor&lt;/i&gt; and the portion of <i data-commentator="Mishnah Berurah" data-label="טו"></i> the red heifer are obligatory from <i data-commentator="Turei Zahav" data-order="2"></i> the Torah; therefore residents of villages that <i data-commentator="Ba'er Hetev" data-order="2"></i> <i data-commentator="Sha'arei Teshuvah" data-order="2"></i> lack a minyan must go on those Shabbaths to a place where there is a minyan, in order to hear these readings, which are Torah <i data-commentator="Mishnah Berurah" data-label="טז"></i> obligations: &lt;small&gt;Gloss: And if they cannot come, they should nevertheless be careful to read them with their proper melody and trop [as it is] found written in older sources&lt;/small&gt;:<small> <i data-commentator="Mishnah Berurah" data-label="יז"></i> <i data-commentator="Mishnah Berurah" data-label="יח"></i>]</small>:
